# KNOWN GAP — `Option[T].and_then(closure)` with a METHOD-CALL body emits broken
# code on BOTH lanes, while `gg check` reports clean.
#
#     gg check                 -> OK: no semantic errors   (rc 0, no diagnostic)
#     gg build (C)             -> rc 1
#         error: incompatible types when assigning to type 'int64_t'
#                {aka 'long int'} from type '__gg_Option__int64_t'
#     gg build --backend=llvm  -> rc 1
#         llc: error: '%v23' defined with type 'ptr' but expected 'i64'
#
# Measured at HEAD (2026-08-18).
#
# ⚠ THE DISCRIMINATOR IS THE METHOD-CALL BODY, not `and_then` itself. Control,
# measured working (prints 8):
#     Option[int] o = Some(7)
#     Option[int] r = o.and_then((int b): Some(b + 1))    # bare Some(...) body
# A closure whose body CALLS A METHOD returning `Option[U]` is the failing cell;
# a closure whose body constructs the Option inline is fine.
#
# ⚠ WHY THIS HID BEHIND AN EXISTING ENTRY. `TODO.md`'s bare-`None()` residual
# entry states that `.and_then/.flat_map/.or_else/.map/.map_err` "correctly wrap
# the closure return via `extract_fn_return_type`". That is TRUE AT TYPECHECK —
# which is exactly why this defect is invisible there and only surfaces in the
# emitted code. The two are different layers, not the same bug; do not merge.
# Also distinct from the filed SELF-HOST combinator segfault (different lane) and
# from the ggdef combinator subset gap (different axis).
#
# The C message names the shape precisely: an `__gg_Option__int64_t` is being
# assigned into an `int64_t` slot, i.e. the closure's `Option` return is being
# unwrapped one level too early — or the destination slot was typed from the
# payload rather than from the closure's declared return.
#
# INTENDED: this builds on both lanes and prints `7`.
#
# Wired test: known_gap_option_and_then_method_call_body (tests/integration.rs,
# #[ignore]d), asserting the INTENDED output. It fails today and goes green when
# the closure's `Option` return type reaches the destination slot.
struct Box2:
    int v

equip Box2:
    Option[int] pick(self):
        return Some(self.v)

void main():
    Option[Box2] o = Some(Box2(7))
    Option[int] r = o.and_then((Box2 b): b.pick())
    print(r.unwrap())
